\startchapter[reference=conclusion, title={Conclusion}]

Single-cell RNA-Seq has grown to be an extensive and active field of science, with new challenges coming up through integration of different kinds of data and as consequence of ever-growing dataset sizes.
Going up to hundreds of thousands of cells, the data allows us to abandon the notion of discrete cell types and to explore the continuous processes at play.
Destiny and the gene relevance method presented in this thesis prove to be valuable contributions in this direction.
\TODO[can I write that?!]

% extensibility and openness
As demands on reliability at scale grow along demands posed to algorithms, more practices from software engineering are finding their way into biological science.
For example, extensible and efficient data format standards are born from collaboration of scientists to supplant the ad-hoc text-based formats we used to incompatibly modify to suit situational needs.
Open source analysis frameworks have been designed to offer an open and welcoming environment for new as well as established analysts and method developers.
My contributions towards more reproducible, scalable and interconnectible scientific programming have furthered these changes along.

\startsection[title={Multi-resolution scRNA-Seq Analysis}]
The analysis of continuous processes driving cellular heterogeneity has been enabled by fluorescent tagging and single cell imaging.
While careful selection of parameters like sequencing depth is vital, scRNA-Seq is an unbiased and inexpensive method able to detect rare cells \cite[torre2018].
This makes it uniquely suited for heterogeneity analyses.
A specific property of cellular heterogeneity that is rarely addressed is its multi-resolution nature.

Analysis algorithms for tasks in this area have evolved alongside the protocols able to sequence hundreds of thousands of cells.
Suitable for the low sample sizes of bulk RNA-Seq, simple linear dimension reduction methods like PCA were used to visualize early single cell studies.
Nonlinear methods like diffusion maps, t-SNE or later UMAP have been developed and continue being improved to fit the nature of transcriptome data.
But while they capture both continuity and multi-resolution features, their output does not contain directly interpretable inferences about processes at different resolutions.
Methods like the growing multitude of trajectory inference algorithms or gene relevance complement them in the same way \cite[saelens2019].
Of all methods known to the author, only \n{PAGA} so far distinctively dissects multi-resolution heterogeneity \cite[wolf2019].
To analyze this foundational attribute of cellular processes, a multi-resolution variant of gene relevance would be of great use.
\stopsection

\startsection[title={\n{Scanpy} Beyond scRNA-Seq Analysis}]
Recent developments in the joint measurement of scRNA-Seq data and multiple other data types at single cell resolution have enabled multimodal analysis.
With this ripe opportunity for analysis development, Nature Methods has named single-cell multimodal omics its method of the year 2019 \cite[nmeth2020].
\inlinecite[efremova2020] provide a review of the different kinds of single cell data that became available in short succession.

\n{Scanpy} and \n{AnnData} have partial support for these kinds of data, but lack a true multimodal mode where different kinds of features can be stored for one set of observations.
Analyses not relying on a mode like this are already supported.
% transfer learning
If there is no 1:1 relation between samples, transfer learning can be used to migrate labels from one dataset to another \cite[abdelaal2019].
This technique has been successfully employed to link data in and to the \n{Human Cell Atlas} \cite[regev2017].
% RNA Velocity
RNA Velocity is the method of using information from unspliced pre-mRNA to infer trajectories of cell differentiation \cite[lamanno2018].
With \n{scVelo}, an user friendly implementation and extension of the model based on \n{Scanpy} and \n{AnnData} exists \cite[bergen2019].
% spatial
Spatial analysis in \n{Scanpy} is similarly straightforward.
The current development of spatial conventions and plotting routines will encourage the development of \n{Scanpy} based spatial algorithms similar to the R tools trendsceek \cite[edsgärd2018] and SpatialDE \cite[svensson2018].
\TODO[mention the already-supported data types in methods/intro instead?]

For data containing multiple high-dimensional feature modes for the same cells, a multimodal \n{AnnData} will substantially increase usability.
% epigenetics
This includes epigenetic data \cite[henriksson2019].
Chromatin accessibility on single cell genomes has been possible to measure for some years already using single-cell Assay for Transposase-Accessible Chromatin using Sequencing (scATAC-Seq, \inlinecite[buenrostro2013]).
DNA methylation can be measured now using the recently developed single cell Cleavage Under Targets and Tagmentation (scCUT&Tag, \inlinecite[kaya-okur2019]).
For analysis of scRNA-Seq data combined with both kinds of data, \n{epiScanpy} offers a \n{scanpy}-integrated workflow \cite[danese2019].
Since it uses the current version of \n{AnnData}, its API will benefit from the upcoming multimodal extension of \n{AnnData}.

Another multimodal data type is scRNA-Seq with surface protein abundances.
Despite the difficulties in measuring proteins, it is necessary in cases where transcriptomics and proteomics are poorly correlated, such as with posttranslationally modified proteins.
Measuring those abundances together with scRNA-Seq data has become possible through Cellular Indexing of Transcriptomes and Epitopes by sequencing (CITE-seq) \cite[gayoso2019].

